    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Airlines Flying Nonstop from London to Dubai</h2>
      <p>The London–Dubai route is dominated by <strong>Emirates</strong>, which operates 4–5 daily departures from LHR to DXB. The airline's A380 and 777 fleet on this route offers one of the most competitive in-flight products in the industry.</p>
      <p><strong>British Airways</strong> competes daily with its own nonstop service, operating Club World business class and standard economy. BA is the preferred option for oneworld alliance members and Avios point holders.</p>
      <p><strong>flydubai</strong> operates nonstop service from Gatwick (LGW) at budget-carrier prices. This is often the cheapest nonstop option from London to Dubai for economy travelers.</p>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>💡 Emirates frequently releases sale fares on this route — sometimes as low as £300–400 return in Economy. Set a Skyscanner alert and you will be notified the moment prices drop.</p></div>
      <h2>Tips for London to Dubai Nonstop</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Winter in Dubai (November–March) is peak season. Book 3–5 months ahead for this period.</li>
        <li>Emirates sale fares on this route are common — January and September are historically the best months to find deals.</li>
        <li>If you are flying onward to Asia or Africa from Dubai, Emirates' transit hotel (in the terminal) offers free or discounted rooms for longer layovers.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Terminal Guide: London and Dubai</h2>
      <p><strong>At London Heathrow (LHR):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Terminal 3 (T3):</strong> Emirates departs and arrives at T3. The Emirates lounge at T3 is one of Heathrow's best — accessible to Business and First Class passengers and Skywards Gold members.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 5 (T5):</strong> British Airways exclusively. Modern terminal with dedicated First Wing entrance for premium cabin passengers. BA's First and Club lounges are in T5.</li>
        <li><strong>London Gatwick South (LGW):</strong> flydubai uses the South Terminal at Gatwick. The Gatwick Express to Victoria takes 30 minutes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>At Dubai International Airport (DXB):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Terminal 3 (T3):</strong> Emirates arrivals and departures. T3 is the world's largest airport terminal by floor space. Emirates' First Class lounge at T3 features a spa and a la carte dining.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 2 (T2):</strong> flydubai arrives and departs from T2. Smaller and more basic. Note that T3 and T2 are not directly connected — allow extra time if connecting between the two.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="info-box"><p>ℹ️ Dubai International Airport is one of the world's busiest, particularly at peak hours. Allow 3 hours before departure for international check-in. Emirates check-in typically opens 3.5 hours before departure for T3 flights.</p></div>
      <h2>Baggage Allowances by Airline</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Emirates Economy (Saver fare):</strong> 1 × 23kg checked bag included. 7kg carry-on included. Flex fare includes 2 × 23kg.</li>
        <li><strong>British Airways Economy:</strong> 1 × 23kg checked bag included on World Traveller and above. Lite/Basic fares exclude checked bags but include one piece of hand baggage.</li>
        <li><strong>flydubai Economy:</strong> Basic fares include a 7kg carry-on only. A 20kg checked bag allowance purchased at booking costs approximately £30–50 each way. Always buy bags at booking — airport charges are significantly higher.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Best Time to Book London to Dubai Nonstop</h2>
      <p><strong>Cheapest periods:</strong> April through early June (after UK Easter, before peak summer). September through early October. These windows typically offer the best economy fares on this route, with prices sometimes 35–40% below the winter peak.</p>
      <p><strong>Most expensive:</strong> December through January (Dubai winter peak coincides with UK Christmas travel). February half-term. UK summer school holidays (late July to early September).</p>
      <p><strong>Emirates sale fare timing:</strong> Emirates typically releases promotional fares in January and September. Flash sales appear with 4–8 weeks lead time. Set a Skyscanner alert on this route and you will be notified immediately when a sale launches.</p>
      <h2>Visa and Entry Requirements</h2>
      <p><strong>UK citizens visiting Dubai:</strong> UK passport holders receive a free 30-day visa on arrival at Dubai International. No advance application is required. Passport must be valid for at least 6 months beyond the entry date. UK citizens can also apply for a 30-day or 90-day visa in advance through the Dubai tourism website or UAE official channels.</p>
      <p><strong>UAE nationals traveling to the UK:</strong> UAE passport holders require a UK Standard Visitor Visa. Apply through the UK Visas and Immigration portal online. Standard processing is 3 weeks; priority services available. A biometric appointment at a UK Visa Application Centre is required.</p>
      <p><strong>Other EU/EEA nationals:</strong> Most European nationalities can enter Dubai visa-free or with a visa on arrival for 30–90 days depending on passport. Check the UAE GDRFA website for your specific nationality.</p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the best cabin class on Emirates for this route?</h3>
      <p>Economy on the A380 (3-4-3 configuration on lower deck, 2-4-2 on upper deck) is significantly more comfortable than on the 777 (3-3-3 configuration). Check the aircraft type before booking and request an A380 service if available. Business Class (lie-flat, direct aisle access in 2-2-2 configuration) is excellent for the 7-hour flight. First Class private suites are available on select A380 services and are considered among the world's best First Class products.</p>
      <h3>How many Emirates flights operate from London to Dubai each day?</h3>
      <p>Emirates operates 4–5 daily departures from Heathrow to DXB, making it one of the most frequently served international routes from the UK. Specific departure times vary — check emiratescom for the current schedule as it changes seasonally.</p>
      <h3>Is Gatwick or Heathrow better for flying to Dubai?</h3>
      <p>For full-service travel (Emirates or BA), Heathrow is the better choice — more flights, better airline lounge access, and more convenient connections to central London. For price-focused travelers on flydubai, Gatwick is the only option and can offer significant savings. Gatwick connects to London via the Gatwick Express (30 min to Victoria) or Thameslink to Blackfriars and beyond.</p>
      <h3>Can I get a free stopover in Dubai on this route?</h3>
      <p>Emirates operates a complimentary stopover program on qualifying fares for passengers continuing onward to another destination on the same ticket. However, on point-to-point London–Dubai tickets, this does not apply. If you want to spend time in Dubai before flying onward, book your itinerary as London–Dubai–[final destination] on a single Emirates ticket to qualify for stopover programs.</p>
      <h2>In-Flight Experience Comparison</h2>
      <p><strong>Emirates Economy (A380 lower deck):</strong> 17.5-inch seat width in 3-4-3, 31-inch pitch. ICE entertainment system with 4,500+ channels. Complimentary meals, wine, beer, and soft drinks. USB charging. One of the best full-service economy products available on this route.</p>
      <p><strong>British Airways World Traveller (777/787):</strong> 17-inch seat width, 31-inch pitch on most aircraft. Updated entertainment on newer 787 services. Meal included. Generally rated below Emirates for economy comfort and catering but preferred by BA loyalists and Avios holders.</p>
      <p><strong>flydubai Economy (737 MAX):</strong> Narrow-body aircraft — tighter cabin than widebody alternatives. 30-inch pitch in standard rows. Meals purchased separately. Entertainment via personal device streaming. Best suited to price-focused travelers comfortable with a basic 7-hour service.</p>
